british drivers are accelerating towards  a carbon-free  future a ban on the sale of new petrol  and diesel cars  will arrive 10 years earlier than  planned so from 2030  charging stations like this in east  london will need to be the norm  not the exception  there is a long way to go it's estimated  22 million  charging points are needed just one in  six councils has installed any on-street  and a third of households have no  off-street parking  but the industry is confident it can  catch up the infrastructure is coming  and it will certainly be in place ready  for 2030. it's it's challenging in  places to install those charges  in every location but the key thing is  we need a balance of charging at home  for those who have driveways charging at  work and then lots of  adequate public charging too fast  charging points like these are an  important part of establishing a truly  national charging network but there's a  problem they require  a huge amount of power have a look at  this the power is coming into them from  this  red box it's been fed out in these  black cables but just look at the cable  that's bringing the power in  this huge black line runs all the way  back  to an electricity substation outside the  car park there's going to be huge  new demand on the national power network  if electric vehicles are going to be  truly viable in a decade  we're going to have to get smarter about  how we use power particularly at home  and this is what it might look like  these are some of the greenest council  houses in the country  built off-site in yorkshire by ilka and  assembled in woolwich  with solar panels feeding power back  into the grid  and an air source heat pump 12 times  less polluting than  gas laura and salius love it  if you can afford of course i believe  everyone wants to have this but because  it's not cheap  and well you you need to have finance to  to put those panels on and this to  to change all system to new system but  if everyone can do this why not i  recommend to everyone this every homes  heating will have to be transformed  and converting to hydrogen-powered  boilers and even entire communities  like this test site in cumbria will be  part of the answer  this is what the future looks like this  is what northern gas networks and the  other  industry players are really striving  towards to transition our network  to move hydrogen and move away from  natural gas  the government hopes it's laying the  groundwork for a greener future  we'll all notice the change if it's to  work  paul kelso sky news
 

